  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential strong storms Friday. Damaging winds\nand localized flooding possible.\n\nStorms have already begun to develop west of the Blue Ridge and\ncoverage of storms will likely increase through this evening in\nresponse to a subtle wave and a slow-moving frontal boundary\nwithin the region. Moderate surface heating and increasing dew \npoints into the upper 60s has resulted in MLCAPE/SBCAPE now \napproaching 1500 J/kg. Further destabilization throughout the \nremainder of the afternoon will allow storms to increase in \ncoverage across the mountains. PWAT values are not particularly \nhigh (around 1.50\" or less) but nearly stationary storms will\nstill pose a localized flooding threat through this evening.\nShear remains weak, so organized convection remains a lower\nrisk, but multicell clusters and cell mergers later this\nafternoon/evening may introduce a damaging wind threat for a \nfew hours.\n\nFriday, the front slows and stalls somewhere across the VA/NC\nPiedmont/Foothills, as several vorts track from the TN Valley to\nthe east coast. Looks like better severe threat appears to stay\neast of the mountains, with more instability but will depend on\ncloud coverage early in the day. If sunshine is plentiful expect\nmoderate to potentially strong instability east/southeast of the\nfront where upper 60s to low 70s dewpoints will be present. \nLapse rates will be quite weak, but most guidance shows \ntemperatures warming into the low to mid 80s which should \nprovide pockets of moderate to strong instability. Any storms \nthat can take advantage of the a stronger flow aloft may be \nlonger- lived and capable of some damaging wind gusts. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat builds into next week.\n\nA strong upper ridge begins to build by early next week. Above\nnormal temperatures appear likely as the ridge remains in place\nthrough at least the middle of next week. As such with strong \nridging, rain/storm chances will become limited.",
